The Nautilus DevOps team is automating VPC creation using Terraform to manage networking efficiently. As part of this task, they need to create a VPC with specific requirements.

For this task, create an AWS VPC using Terraform with the following requirements:

The VPC name datacenter-vpc should be stored in a variable named KKE_vpc.

The VPC should have a CIDR block of 10.0.0.0/16.

Note:

The configuration values should be stored in a variables.tf file.

The Terraform script should be structured with a main.tf file referencing variables.tf.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform.

Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To satisfy this task, you need two files: variables.tf (holding the configuration values) and main.tf (referencing those variables). 
The VPC name must be stored in a variable named exactly KKE_vpc.

📄 /home/bob/terraform/variables.tf
hcl
variable "KKE_vpc" {
  description = "Name of the VPC"
  type        = string
  default     = "datacenter-vpc"
}

variable "KKE_vpc_cidr" {
  description = "CIDR block for the VPC"
  type        = string
  default     = "10.0.0.0/16"
}
📄 /home/bob/terraform/main.tf
hcl
resource "aws_vpc" "datacenter_vpc" {
  cidr_block = var.KKE_vpc_cidr

  tags = {
    Name = var.KKE_vpc
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Variable KKE_vpc: Holds the VPC name datacenter-vpc, exactly as required. It's referenced in the Name tag so the VPC appears with the correct name in AWS.

Variable KKE_vpc_cidr: Holds the CIDR block 10.0.0.0/16. This keeps all configuration values in variables.tf, as the task instructs.

main.tf references: var.KKE_vpc and var.KKE_vpc_cidr — no hardcoded values in main.tf, keeping the script properly structured.

Region: Handled by your existing provider.tf (us-east-1), so no provider block is needed here.

🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Confirm No Changes
bash
terraform plan
It should return:

text
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
🔍 Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-vpcs \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=datacenter-vpc" \
  --query "Vpcs[].{CIDR:CidrBlock,Name:Tags[?Key=='Name']|[0].Value}"
You should see the VPC with CIDR 10.0.0.0/16 and name datacenter-vpc, confirming both variables were applied correctly.
